If an approximable map h commutes with endomaps f and g and sends the bottom element to the bottom element, then the image under h of the closure of any stage of a fixed-point chain built from tokens of f lies below the least fixed point of g.

The proof is by induction on the stage. At stage zero the chain set is empty, so its closure is the bottom element, and strictness sends that element to the bottom element, which lies below the least fixed point. At a successor stage the chain supplies a previous set and an entailment from a finite set of tokens of f. Those tokens lie in the relation of f, so f relates their input union to their output union and, by monotonicity, relates the previous set to the current set. The closure of the current set therefore lies below the image under f of the closure of the previous set. Monotonicity of h, the commuting equation on elements, monotonicity of g, and the fixed-point equation for g give the inequality.
